language variant
language en-earth
name voicebox
gender male 45

// VOICEBOX — the 1990s speaking machine, with a passport.
//
// This is the voice of a hardware speech box: the sound of formant synthesis
// before anyone had the compute to hide that it was formant synthesis. Not a
// parody of one — every choice below is what those machines actually did, and
// why they sounded the way they did.
//
// It sits on `en-earth`, the world-English base, so the accent belongs to
// nowhere in particular. That is the point of the character: a machine voice has
// no hometown, and a box built to be understood everywhere was tuned to the
// vowels several Englishes AGREE on rather than to any one of them.

// A NARROW BAND, HELD. Those machines had a pitch generator, not a larynx: the
// contour was computed, so it moved in steps and it moved very little. 118 is
// mid-male — deliberately unremarkable, because the character is in the TIMBRE
// and a distinctive pitch would only distract from it. The range of 22 is enough
// that it is not a monotone drone and far too little to sound alive.
pitch 118 140

// THE GIVEAWAY IS THE FORMANTS, AND THEY ARE STEADY.
// A real vocal tract is always moving, so its resonances smear. A formant
// synthesiser holds each one at the target it was told, and the ear hears that
// steadiness as machinery. The peaks are strong and narrow through F1-F3 (the
// band that decides which vowel you heard) and fall off a cliff above F5,
// because those boxes had a fixed number of resonators and simply stopped.
formant 0 100 122 120
formant 1 106 126 128
formant 2 112 128 126
formant 3 110 120 118
formant 4 104 108  96
formant 5  92  94  60
formant 6  74  78  26
formant 7  64  70  14
formant 8  58  64   8

// THE BUZZ IS THE POINT, AND IT LIVES AT 1 kHz.
// A hard peak in the upper-mid with very little above 3 kHz is exactly the
// spectral shape of a small speaker being driven by a pulse train. The bottom is
// deliberately thin — those boxes had no chest, and adding one would make this a
// person doing an impression instead of a machine.
tone 300 150 1000 250 2000 175 3400 90

// NO BREATH AT ALL. This is the single line that separates a machine from a
// person: every human voice leaks air, and this one does not. voicing is pinned
// high and flat, flutter is zero, roughness is zero. Nothing here is irregular,
// because nothing here is biological.
breath 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0
breathw 40 40 40 40 40 40 40 40
voicing 100
flutter 0
roughness 0

// OVER-ARTICULATED, WHICH IS WHY YOU COULD UNDERSTAND THEM OVER A PHONE LINE.
// Those machines were built for announcements and accessibility, so consonants
// were pushed hard — every stop released, every fricative full length. It is the
// most machine-like thing about the voice and also the reason it is easy to
// follow.
consonants 98 96

// A SMALL HARD ROOM: the cabinet the speaker was mounted in. Short enough to
// read as a box rather than a hall, and it is a resonance, not an echo.
echo 32 14

// STRESS BY LOUDNESS, IN STEPS.
// The contour was quantised, so emphasis arrives as a level change rather than
// as a curve. stressAdd moves in even increments — no easing, no overshoot,
// nothing a voice would actually do — and stressAmp is nearly flat, because
// those boxes had one volume and used it.
intonation 2
stressAmp   15 15 16 16 17 17 18 18
stressAdd    0 10 20 30 40 50 60 70
